---
// "Who can read your data": the custody statement for /security, on your
// computer, on a multi-user server you run, and on a managed instance. The
// statement is published as written; change its wording here and nowhere else.
// The beta features it describes are read from the truth data, so a status
// change shows up in the headings on the next build.
import { feature } from '../../lib/truth/read';

const server = feature('server.multi-user');
feature('vault.member-secrets', ['beta']);
feature('access.audit-log', ['beta']);
feature('vault.passphrase', ['shipped']);

const MANAGED = { exportBusinessDays: 5, deleteDays: 30 };
---

<h3 id="on-your-computer">On your computer</h3>
<p>
	Ikenga keeps your projects, artifacts, terminal history and agent sessions on your computer. Keys are kept in a
	passphrase vault. Your coding engine still sends your prompts to its own model provider, under that provider's terms.
</p>

<h3 id="on-your-server">On a multi-user server you run{server.status === 'beta' && ' (beta)'}</h3>
<ul class="lim-list">
	<li>
		Members of one organisation share the server and are trusted with each other. It is not built to keep untrusted
		tenants apart.
	</li>
	<li>
		Each person's secrets are kept apart from everyone else's in the app. They are sealed under a key that the server
		holds, so agents can keep working when the person is not signed in.
	</li>
	<li>
		That means whoever controls the server can read what is on it. The server runs as root on its Linux host, so anyone
		with root access to that host can read every member's files, secrets and agent sessions. Give that access only to
		people you would trust with all of it.
	</li>
	<li>
		Back up the server's key separately from the server. A lost key cannot be recreated, and there is no command to
		rotate it yet.
	</li>
	<li>
		The audit log records sign-ins, pairings, role changes, approvals and dispatches in a hash chain you can verify and
		export. It helps you spot changes. Removing only the newest entries may not be detected, and it does not stop
		someone with root access to the host from rewriting it.
	</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="managed">On a managed instance (not available yet)</h3>
<p>
	When we run a dedicated server for your organisation, Ikenga staff are its operator. The server holds the key to your
	secrets, so <strong>we can technically read your code, files and secrets on it.</strong> We will not offer a managed
	instance until these controls are in place, and a managed contract will include them:
</p>
<ul class="lim-list">
	<li>
		Only named Ikenga operators can reach the host, and only to fix a problem you reported, respond to an incident on
		your instance, or apply an upgrade.
	</li>
	<li>Every operator sign-in to the host is recorded, and you can see those records on request.</li>
	<li>
		Your data can be exported on request within {MANAGED.exportBusinessDays} business days. The instance and its
		backups are deleted within {MANAGED.deleteDays} days of the contract ending.
	</li>
	<li>
		A data processing agreement, and a list of the subprocessors, such as the server provider, that handle your data.
	</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="model-access">Model access</h3>
<p>
	On a server we run, your engines use credentials you supply: an API key, or your own Amazon Bedrock or Google Cloud
	route. We never copy a personal subscription sign-in onto a server we run. Ikenga includes no model usage.
</p>

<h3 id="certifications">Certifications</h3>
<p><strong>No SOC 2 yet; security questionnaire on request.</strong></p>
<p>
	Ikenga has no SOC 2 report or other certification yet. We answer a standard security questionnaire, CAIQ Lite, on
	request.
</p>

<style>
	h3 {
		margin: var(--space-10) 0 0;
		font: 600 var(--text-h3) / var(--lead-h3) var(--font-body);
		color: var(--fg);
	}
	.lim-list {
		list-style: none;
		margin: var(--space-4) 0 0;
		padding: 0;
		display: grid;
		gap: var(--space-3);
		max-width: 62ch;
		color: var(--fg-muted);
	}
	.lim-list li {
		display: flex;
		gap: var(--space-3);
		align-items: baseline;
	}
	.lim-list li::before {
		content: '';
		width: 6px;
		height: 6px;
		border-radius: 1px;
		background: var(--chip-carve);
		flex: none;
		transform: translateY(-2px);
	}
</style>
